  "qualifications": [
    "Both engines keep whole-pixel scroll offsets at device scales 1, 1.5 and 2: every Chromium 156 value in the oracle is an integer, and the pinned WebKit stores ScrollPosition as IntPoint.",
    "The native engine truncates a fractional CSS scroll target toward zero; Chromium rounds to the nearest pixel (20.5 -> 20 vs 21, 10.75 -> 10 vs 11, 7.7 -> 7 vs 8, scrollTop = 11.6 -> 11 vs 12). The same 13 of 16 steps differ on the viewport (Window and scrolling-element routes) and on a scrolling box at every scale; the step list and values are under qualifiedSteps.",
    "Event differences follow from the positions: a step whose truncated position is unchanged dispatches no scroll or scrollend natively (scrollTo(10.75, 20.75) after (10, 20); scrollTo(10.4, 20.4) after (10, 20); scrollBy({top: 0.6}) from (5, 6)), and a step that moves natively but not in Chromium dispatches scroll and scrollend natively (scrollBy(-0.3, -0.3) from (5, 6) -> (4, 5); scrollBy(-0.5, -0.5) from the maximum -> one pixel less).",
    "Element.scrollTop/scrollLeft are WebIDL long in the pinned WebKit (webkit.org/b/188045): the setter takes int32, and the caller converts a CSS value with ToInt32 truncation.",
    "A smooth scrollTo dispatched 3 scroll events then scrollend natively and 4 then scrollend in Chromium; the count follows each engine's animation frames. The final position differs by the truncation rule ((0, 50) vs (1, 51)).",
    "Clamping at 0 and at the maximum is equal (viewport 1680x1761, box 900x901 at every scale), as are maxima, scrollWidth/scrollHeight minus client size, and every unqualified step's value and events."
